40: import static ratpack.handling.Handlers.chain;
41: 
42: /**
43:  * Static utility methods for creating Google Guice based Ratpack infrastructure.
44:  * <p>
45:  * Any non trivial application will require supporting objects for the handler implementations, for persistence for example.
46:  * These supporting objects can be managed by Guice and made available to handlers (either by dependency injection or registry lookup)
47:  * for increased reusability, modularity and testability.
48:  * <p>
49:  * The Guice integration is not part of the Ratpack core library, but is available as a separate add on.
50:  * That said, Ratpack is designed to support something like Guice as an integral piece via it's {@link Registry} abstraction.
51:  * Guice is the “official” solution.
52:  * </p>
53:  * <h3>Starting a Guice Ratpack app</h3>
54:  * <p>
55:  * The user entry point for Ratpack applications is the {@link ratpack.launch.HandlerFactory} implementation
56:  * that is given to the {@link ratpack.launch.LaunchConfigBuilder#build(ratpack.launch.HandlerFactory)} method.
57:  * This implementation can use one of the {@code handler(...) } static methods.
58:  * <p>
59:  * Below is a complete example for bootstrapping a Guice based Ratpack application.
60:  * </p>
61:  * <pre class="tested">
62:  * import ratpack.handling.*;
63:  * import ratpack.registry.*;
64:  * import ratpack.guice.*;
65:  * import ratpack.launch.*;
66:  * import ratpack.func.*;
67:  * import com.google.inject.*;
68:  * import javax.inject.*;
69:  *
70:  * // Some service to be dependency injected
71:  * class SomeService {}
72:  *
73:  * // A Guice module that provides the service
74:  * class ServiceModule extends AbstractModule {
75:  *   protected void configure() {
76:  *     bind(SomeService.class);
77:  *   }
78:  * }
79:  *
80:  * // An action that registers the module with the registry, making it part of the application
81:  * class Bindings implements Action&lt;BindingsSpec&gt; {
82:  *   public void execute(BindingsSpec bindings) {
83:  *     bindings.add(new ServiceModule());
84:  *   }
85:  * }
86:  *
87:  * // A handler implementation that is dependency injected
88:  * {@literal @}Singleton
89:  * class InjectedHandler implements Handler {
90:  *   private final SomeService service;
91:  *
92:  *   {@literal @}Inject
93:  *   public InjectedHandler(SomeService service) {
94:  *     this.service = service;
95:  *   }
96:  *
97:  *   public void handle(Context exchange) {
98:  *     // …
99:  *   }
100:  * }
101:  *
102:  * // A chain configurer that adds a dependency injected handler to the chain
103:  * class HandlersBootstrap implements Action&lt;Chain&gt; {
104:  *   public void execute(Chain chain) {
105:  *
106:  *     // The registry in a Guice backed chain can be used to retrieve objects that were bound,
107:  *     // or to create objects that are bound “just-in-time”.
108:  *     Registry&lt;Object&gt; registry = chain.getRegistry();
109:  *     Handler injectedHandler = registry.get(InjectedHandler.class);
110:  *
111:  *     // Add the handler into the chain
112:  *     chain.get("some/path", injectedHandler);
113:  *   }
114:  * }
115:  *
116:  * // A HandlerFactory implementation used to bootstrap the application
117:  * class MyHandlerFactory implements HandlerFactory {
118:  *   public Handler create(LaunchConfig launchConfig) {
119:  *     return Guice.chain(launchConfig, new Bindings(), new HandlersBootstrap());
120:  *   }
121:  * }
122:  *
123:  * // Building a launch config with our handler factory
124:  * LaunchConfig launchConfig = LaunchConfigBuilder.baseDir(new File("appRoot")).build(new MyHandlerFactory());
125:  * </pre>
126:  * <h3>Accessing Guice bound objects in Handlers</h3>
127:  * <p>
128:  * There are two ways to use Guice bound objects in your handler implementations.
129:  * </p>
130:  * <h4>Dependency Injected Handlers</h4>
131:  * <p>
132:  * The {@code handler()} methods used to create a Guice backed application take an {@link Action} that operates on a {@link Chain} instance.
133:  * This chain instance given to this action provides a Guice backed {@link Registry} via its {@link Chain#getRegistry()} method.
134:  * This registry is able to retrieve objects that were explicitly bound (i.e. defined by a module), and bind objects “just in time”.
135:  * This means that it can be used to construct dependency injected {@link Handler} implementations.
136:  * </p>
137:  * <p>
138:  * Simply pass the class of the handler implementation to create a new dependency injected instance of to this method,
139:  * then add it to the chain.
140:  * </p>
141:  * <p>
142:  * See the code above for an example of this.
143:  * </p>
144:  * <h4>Accessing dependencies via context registry lookup</h4>
145:  * <p>
146:  * The {@link ratpack.handling.Context} object that is given to a handler's {@link Handler#handle(ratpack.handling.Context)}
147:  * method is also a registry implementation. In a Guice backed app, Guice bound objects can be retrieved via the {@link Registry#get(Class)} method of
148:  * the context. You can retrieve any bound object via its publicly bound type.
149:  * </p>
150:  * <p>
151:  * However, this will not create “just-in-time” bindings. Only objects that were explicitly bound can be retrieved this way.
152:  * </p>
153:  * <h3>Guice modules as Ratpack “plugins”.</h3>
154:  * <p>
155:  * Add on Ratpack functionality is typically provided via Guice modules.
156:  * For example, the <a href="https://github.com/FasterXML/jackson-databind">Jackson</a> integration for JSON serialisation
157:  * is provided by the {@code ratpack-jackson} add-on which ships a Guice module.
158:  * To use its functionality simply register the module it provides with the {@link BindingsSpec} used to bootstrap the application.
159:  * </p>
160:  * <h3>Groovy Applications</h3>
161:  * <p>
162:  * The Ratpack Groovy add-on provides application modes that automatically incorporate Guice (namely the “Ratpack Script” mode).
163:  * The module registration process is simpler and more convenient in this mode, and there are additional options for obtaining
164:  * Guice bound objects.
165:  * </p>
166:  * <p>
167:  * See the Groovy add-on's documentation for more details.
168:  * </p>
169:  */
